The third point is that two or more tasks, or even two stages, often merge. In one session a client may name parts of a problem, set goals and develop strategies. New and more serious concerns may surface while goals are being set, and the process then moves back to an earlier, exploratory stage. Helping is seldom a linear event.

Egan gives the words of a client discussing a troubled relationship with a friend. The client says that every kind gesture is thrown back at him, so perhaps being more considerate is not the answer. Maybe he is a wimp rather than the self-centred person she says he is; maybe he is being a wimp with the counsellor too, and the counsellor is allowing it; maybe it is time to pursue his own agenda instead of fitting into everyone else's plans; he needs a closer look at the person he wants to be in relationships.

Egan asks readers to look closely. In a few sentences this client:
- mentions a failed action strategy (being nicer);
- questions a goal set earlier (being more considerate);
- hints at a new problem (being a "wimp");
- suggests a difficulty in the helping relationship itself;
- offers, at least in general terms, a different approach to managing the problem (looking after his own interests); and
- recasts the problem as an opportunity to develop a more solid interpersonal style.

The helper's challenge is to make sense of the client's entry points and guide the client through whichever stage or task will move them towards problem-managing and opportunity-developing action.
